WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:13.759
This is a paper really designed to see from
the broader view as to how we can best present

00:00:13.759 --> 00:00:21.000
some very complex information about the quality
of delivery of NHS England as an organisation,

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.010
and indeed also about the quality of delivery
of the NHS as a system.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:32.570
If you turn to page two, this report is trying
to capture data that will help support the

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:40.609
Board's ability to hold the team to account
across a range of different sorts of measures.

00:00:40.609 --> 00:00:44.510
We have in our business plan, 11 core metrics.

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:47.760
This is probably the first standard reporting.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.920
We also, of course, are very interested in
the degree to which we are delivering on our

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:51.920
mandate obligations.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:56.899
We are very focused on the degree to which
NHS performance is responding to our commissioning

00:00:56.899 --> 00:00:58.699
activity, and so on.

00:00:58.699 --> 00:01:03.290
I say that to give you a sense of the variety
of different indicators that we will want

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:05.890
to consider as a Board.

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:09.790
I would like to make two other points before
I pass onto other colleagues to talk about

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:11.810
the substance of the report.

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:16.960
We are limited by the degree to which we have
data that actually actively and in real time,

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:23.160
or in useful frequency, supports our ability
to judge outcome and performance.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.460
Therefore, we have divided this report into
two parts.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:30.890
There is the first part which is: where we
have data that usefully supports operational

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:37.720
insight and where there is data but it is
frankly so historic that it is of no real

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:41.490
use to the Board currently in determining
whether NHS England is having any impact.

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:46.730
We published that historic data in section
E. That historic data is a baseline against

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:48.680
which we can in future be held to account.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:51.940
I just want to make that point that we are
limited by the amount of data.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:55.820
Clearly, as you know and as I have said in
previous Board meetings, we have a massive

00:01:55.820 --> 00:02:01.750
programme to hopefully very quickly transform
the infrastructure we have to enable much

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:04.160
more data to flow.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:07.570
Obviously, that takes some time to deliver,
but we have made some good progress.

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:09.929
For now, we have a limit on the data.

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:15.489
The invitation to the Board is to make comments
on how best we can present this complexity

00:02:15.489 --> 00:02:18.760
of information in a way that it is going to
be most useful to Board members.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:24.719
If I just perhaps pass onto Barbara to pick
up on some of the substantive points, which

00:02:24.719 --> 00:02:26.840
start in paragraph 11.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:28.549
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:02:28.549 --> 00:02:37.900
I would like to draw the Board's attention
particularly to section C, which is the NHS

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:39.560
Performance and Finance Report.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:44.750
I will pass onto Paul when we come onto the
finance aspects of that.

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:50.879
You will see in the narrative that we have
picked out a number of areas in which we thought

00:02:50.879 --> 00:02:57.300
that we both had contemporaneous data and
were critical in terms of the care of our

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:58.999
patient, to focus on for today.

00:02:58.999 --> 00:03:03.819
Clearly, if the Board thinks there are other
areas we should focus on in the future, then

00:03:03.819 --> 00:03:05.040
we will do so.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:13.749
If I can move straight from that narrative
to section C of the Performance and Finance

00:03:13.749 --> 00:03:19.180
Report, there are two distinct areas that
I would particularly like to draw out.

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:22.430
In a little while, I would like to talk about
the 111 service.

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:28.180
First and foremost, I would like to talk about
some of the key performance indicators that

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:30.830
we use for the NHS.

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:37.599
I think it is important to recall how these
indicators came about.

00:03:37.599 --> 00:03:43.950
Patients have rights under the NHS Constitution
and Pledges to many aspects of care, but often

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:47.799
specifically to timely access to their care.

00:03:47.799 --> 00:03:54.931
For example, patients have a right to be treated
within 18 weeks of being referred to the hospital

00:03:54.931 --> 00:03:57.129
by their GP.

00:03:57.129 --> 00:04:02.409
They have a right, if they are suspected of
having cancer, of being diagnosed and treated

00:04:02.409 --> 00:04:04.769
within a much shorter time frame than that.

00:04:04.769 --> 00:04:12.719
They have a right to be either discharged
or admitted from the Accident and Emergency

00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:14.920
department within four hours.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:24.199
There is range of similar targets, operational
standards and quality markers.

00:04:24.199 --> 00:04:29.919
Patients have these rights and we use a proxy
measure to ensure that as a whole, the NHS

00:04:29.919 --> 00:04:36.690
in England is delivering these standards to
the vast majority of our patients.

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:40.310
In certain incidences, there are exceptions
whereby from an individual patient's point

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:47.980
of view, it is better for their safety for
them not necessarily to be admitted or discharged

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:49.099
from Accident and Emergency within four hours.

00:04:49.099 --> 00:04:58.789
That is very small number of patients, and
that is taken account of in the 

00:04:58.789 --> 00:05:00.449
operational standards.

00:05:00.449 --> 00:05:06.530
We make sure that our patients are getting
the right quality of care and not waiting

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:07.810
too long.

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:12.300
One of the most important and topical ones
recently has been the waiting times in Accident

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:19.210
and Emergency departments which you will see
from the graph did dip very significantly

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:27.090
below what we consider to be an operationally
acceptable standard, i.e. that 95% of patients

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:32.419
were either admitted or discharged within
that time frame.

00:05:32.419 --> 00:05:37.569
There is always a dip in the winter.

00:05:37.569 --> 00:05:43.750
It is easier to achieve those levels in the
summer, when there are fewer patients.

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:48.840
On this occasion, we had a more significant
and a more prolonged dip.

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:56.720
You will see now from the dark blue line to
the left of the graph that this operational

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:03.349
standard is now being achieved and has been
achieved for several weeks within the NHS.

00:06:03.349 --> 00:06:10.200
This has been due to a concerted effort from
our commissioners, CCGs, and our providers

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:17.820
in hospitals, supported by joint working between
NHS England, the Trust Development Agency,

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:23.819
Monitor, the Foundation Trust Network and
local government colleagues in order to make

00:06:23.819 --> 00:06:28.759
sure that we have everything in place, so
that we have the right services for those

00:06:28.759 --> 00:06:32.950
patients, and the right services around the
Accident and Emergency department because

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:36.319
often -- of course -- the issue is not about
what is going on in the Accident and Emergency

00:06:36.319 --> 00:06:41.100
department, but rather it is about how many
patients are arriving at an Accident and Emergency

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:45.129
department and sometimes how easy it is for
the patients to be moved from the Accident

00:06:45.129 --> 00:06:47.409
and Emergency department into the right ward
in the hospital.

00:06:47.409 --> 00:06:51.620
That often, of course, depends on the ability
to discharge patients when they are ready

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:52.969
for discharge.

00:06:52.969 --> 00:06:58.259
A concerted effort has, I think, seen performance
come back on track.

00:06:58.259 --> 00:07:03.610
We asked all health communities to come together,
using an urgent care Board.

00:07:03.610 --> 00:07:08.870
This was a group of people across the local
health community -- commissioners and managers

00:07:08.870 --> 00:07:15.830
-- who would be able to look at the day-to-day
issues that were actually blocking patients

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:20.610
swift movement through the Accident and Emergency
department and decide how the whole community

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:22.669
could improvement that service.

00:07:22.669 --> 00:07:27.670
Those urgent care boards not only have helped
get everything back on track for the moment,

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:32.319
but they are particularly looking at winter
planning, because they need to be ready now

00:07:32.319 --> 00:07:34.560
for when we get the next cold snap.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:36.789
That is quite hard to think of on a day like
this.

00:07:36.789 --> 00:07:43.979
They need to plan for the next cold snap of
cold winter and more patients who are more

00:07:43.979 --> 00:07:46.530
ill are arriving in Accident and Emergency.

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:49.669
That is the first indicator I wanted to draw
attention to.

00:07:49.669 --> 00:07:53.360
I think it is worth looking at ambulance performance.

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:58.330
Again patients have rights, depending on the
urgency of their condition, to have an ambulance

00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:01.840
arrive swiftly to support them.

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:07.699
That performance now is excellent and as good
as it has been for some considerable while.

00:08:07.699 --> 00:08:16.419
There are a few pages towards the end on 18-weeks
referral to treatment and cancer rates, for

00:08:16.419 --> 00:08:19.270
which really performance has stayed stable
and stayed good.

00:08:19.270 --> 00:08:24.180
Therefore, I did not intend to talk about
those significantly.

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:29.629
You will perhaps notice on the back page that
we are just looking at the activity.

00:08:29.629 --> 00:08:31.419
We tracked the activity very carefully.

00:08:31.419 --> 00:08:32.950
It is really a performance measure.

00:08:32.950 --> 00:08:40.800
It is a matter of fact, but it helps us to
plan services, both urgent services and elective

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:41.849
care services.

00:08:41.849 --> 00:08:47.862
If I can, rather than going into 111 straight
away, which is slightly different, I might

00:08:47.862 --> 00:08:54.249
just pause there to see if there are any questions
on the key performance indicators.

00:08:54.249 --> 00:09:03.440
&gt;&gt; I think all of this data and the reporting
is a really big step forward.

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:10.389
The two things I think I would be interested
in, if we could find a way to report them

00:09:10.389 --> 00:09:17.130
succinctly without making the reporting too
voluminous, would be to get more of a sense

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:21.790
of the dispersion or the tail.

00:09:21.790 --> 00:09:26.450
These statistics are all about the proportion
that meets a certain standard.

00:09:26.450 --> 00:09:32.300
What we do not see is how many miss it by
a lot.

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:37.031
I do not know what the right way of presenting
that or capturing that is.

00:09:37.031 --> 00:09:45.690
I think that the problems we have been dealing
with are about the tails not about the majority,

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:48.510
and we have got to find some way of reporting
that.

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:56.090
The second thing that would be helpful to
get in the reporting is some sense of regional

00:09:56.090 --> 00:09:57.769
and local variations.

00:09:57.769 --> 00:10:04.540
Is the performance consistent across the country
or is it very different between different

00:10:04.540 --> 00:10:06.050
parts of the country?

00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:12.360
Again, I do not know how you can do that succinctly
without making the reporting voluminous.

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:20.029
It would be relevant in the context of our
obligation on inequalities to be able to see

00:10:20.029 --> 00:10:25.060
something that has a regional flavour to it.

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:29.870
&gt;&gt; Certainly, I am sure that we can look next
time as to how we can bring that information

00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:30.870
back.

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:36.130
It is information that I sit down every week
with TDA and Monitor and look at.

00:10:36.130 --> 00:10:42.570
We are able to see what is happening initially
in all the different hospitals in England

00:10:42.570 --> 00:10:45.140
-- because often this is about acute hospitals.

00:10:45.140 --> 00:10:47.300
There is very significant variation.

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:55.389
A number of our hospitals do very easily meet
this target, week in, week out.

00:10:55.389 --> 00:10:57.690
Some struggle to meet it all the time.

00:10:57.690 --> 00:11:02.459
We see that variation and we are moving to
be able to plot the degree of variation on

00:11:02.459 --> 00:11:06.949
a CCG basis, so that we can start to see which
communities are affected.

00:11:06.949 --> 00:11:09.650
I think that we can look to bring something
like that to you.

00:11:09.650 --> 00:11:12.510
It is a question of how to bring it in a succinct
way.

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:21.149
Also, I think the other thing you were alluding
to was: in a particular instance, what does

00:11:21.149 --> 00:11:22.860
the tail look like?

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:28.440
Yes, we see over 90% of patients within 18
weeks but actually how many patients are waiting

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:32.580
significantly longer than that, how long is
it when it is longer -- I think you are asking

00:11:32.580 --> 00:11:35.950
-- and also, is there a reason?

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:40.700
Again, with all of these, there are instances
when things are in the patient's best interest.

00:11:40.700 --> 00:11:43.199
We could look at a way to do that.

00:11:43.199 --> 00:11:49.570
I would like to assure the Board that in the
operations directive and in the executive

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:54.329
team, we do like at this information on a
regular basis, and we do have it.

00:11:54.329 --> 00:11:58.779
&gt;&gt; Can I just ask a question on Accident and
Emergency?

00:11:58.779 --> 00:12:05.720
Just looking at the graph, I could convince
myself that every year it is worse.

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:09.990
Are we sure that the planning that is being
done for next winter, is not trying to plan

00:12:09.990 --> 00:12:16.759
for last winter again, and that we are assuming
that pressure will be higher?

00:12:16.759 --> 00:12:22.870
What are we actually doing to prepare?

00:12:22.870 --> 00:12:30.370
&gt;&gt; It has become more difficult, year on year,
and in general the number of patients attending

00:12:30.370 --> 00:12:34.290
Accident and Emergency and being admitted
to our hospitals is rising, although it is

00:12:34.290 --> 00:12:41.510
interesting is that the very significant dip
we saw in the last quarter of 2012/2013, the

00:12:41.510 --> 00:12:45.759
total number of patients attending Accident
and Emergency was actually slightly down on

00:12:45.759 --> 00:12:48.550
the quarter of the preceding year, in terms
of admissions.

00:12:48.550 --> 00:12:55.079
We have tried to look quite hard at the reasons
for this in order to be prepared for next

00:12:55.079 --> 00:12:57.589
year, and why it was so off track.

00:12:57.589 --> 00:13:03.380
I think one of the early things that everyone
needs to be aware of is that the patients

00:13:03.380 --> 00:13:09.670
who wait longer than four hours in Accident
and Emergency are generally the poorly patients

00:13:09.670 --> 00:13:10.730
who are to be admitted.

00:13:10.730 --> 00:13:18.890
I think there is a perception that people
wait when they have gone with not a particularly

00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:19.890
serious disorder.

00:13:19.890 --> 00:13:22.110
That is not the case.

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:28.709
Even with the small number that we see, it
is a quite high percentage of the relatively

00:13:28.709 --> 00:13:31.420
small number of patients who are going to
be admitted into hospital.

00:13:31.420 --> 00:13:36.070
It is relevant to look at it against the total
number of patients being admitted rather than

00:13:36.070 --> 00:13:38.620
simply the total number of patients that are
turning up.

00:13:38.620 --> 00:13:41.800
Therefore, when we do miss that time by a
significant margin, it is really genuinely

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:47.540
ill patients who are potentially on trolleys
or in corridors and waiting for Accident and

00:13:47.540 --> 00:13:48.540
Emergency.

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:54.870
Actually, what often happens is that the main
reason is the ability to admit.

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:58.380
It is not that Accident and Emergency cannot
get through the tests and things they need

00:13:58.380 --> 00:13:59.380
to do.

00:13:59.380 --> 00:14:00.970
It sometimes is, but it is mainly the ability
to admit patients.

00:14:00.970 --> 00:14:05.490
Therefore, what we have been trying to look
at, as well as obviously looking to make that

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:10.389
particularly for frail, vulnerable and elderly
people, we have more services available in

00:14:10.389 --> 00:14:14.990
the community and so are improving GP services
and improving out-of-hours services -- and

00:14:14.990 --> 00:14:21.220
these are all the things the urgent care boards
will be looking at -- so that more ill patients

00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:25.259
who really are not very well can be managed
at home which we think is what patients want

00:14:25.259 --> 00:14:26.259
and is best for them.

00:14:26.259 --> 00:14:30.209
We are looking at the systems and processes
within the hospital, because we know that

00:14:30.209 --> 00:14:31.589
makes a huge difference.

00:14:31.589 --> 00:14:36.510
We know that sometimes patients are not discharged
because their tablets are not ready, or because

00:14:36.510 --> 00:14:38.160
they are waiting for a ward round.

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:43.730
We are looking at those processes and particularly
looking at the ability for discharge.

00:14:43.730 --> 00:14:49.730
When we looked at what were the causes of
the dip in quarter four, there were probably

00:14:49.730 --> 00:14:50.730
several causes.

00:14:50.730 --> 00:14:53.579
An increasing number of patients was not one
of them.

00:14:53.579 --> 00:14:59.089
We had a very cold March, which we feel fairly
sure was a contributing factor.

00:14:59.089 --> 00:15:02.000
It was a transition period for the NHS.

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:08.209
It was possible that in response to the Francis
Report people changed the ways and the measures

00:15:08.209 --> 00:15:11.480
of how quickly they could discharge or whether
they ought to admit somebody.

00:15:11.480 --> 00:15:16.839
These may have slightly altered and the perception
of the targets also.

00:15:16.839 --> 00:15:23.290
Another area, of course, was that we all know
that local authority funding has been difficult.

00:15:23.290 --> 00:15:28.060
Actually, one of the possibilities was that
we have not been able to discharge patients

00:15:28.060 --> 00:15:31.930
because the social care they needed was not
available to them.

00:15:31.930 --> 00:15:36.850
We are looking at all of that in order to
make that the best we can possibly make it

00:15:36.850 --> 00:15:37.930
for next winter.

00:15:37.930 --> 00:15:42.779
That is the day-to-day operational stuff,
but of course the urgent care review that

00:15:42.779 --> 00:15:52.149
Bruce is leading starts to look at the more
radical transformation of the service.

00:15:52.149 --> 00:16:00.190
I would like to take everyone to the two slides
on 111.

00:16:00.190 --> 00:16:04.589
I think what we mainly need to record here
is the performance because obviously that

00:16:04.589 --> 00:16:07.600
is what matters to our patients.

00:16:07.600 --> 00:16:11.879
I think that if you look at the performance
data, it is very encouraging indeed.

00:16:11.879 --> 00:16:19.880
We cannot pretend that some providers did
not set off in this service in February and

00:16:19.880 --> 00:16:26.069
March, when they did not have the volumes
of staff and properly trained staff to deal

00:16:26.069 --> 00:16:28.920
with outpatients appropriately.

00:16:28.920 --> 00:16:34.060
We saw some very poor performance with patients
waiting far too long and abandoning calls.

00:16:34.060 --> 00:16:39.500
However, I think you can see that the top
part of the graph actually shows how swiftly

00:16:39.500 --> 00:16:41.410
we are answering calls.

00:16:41.410 --> 00:16:44.860
The red line is our minimum performance.

00:16:44.860 --> 00:16:46.459
It is our quality standard.

00:16:46.459 --> 00:16:53.149
You will see really that performance improved
dramatically since NHS England took on operational

00:16:53.149 --> 00:16:55.370
responsibility in April.

00:16:55.370 --> 00:17:00.379
For the most part now, we meet that major
quality standard of answering nearly all the

00:17:00.379 --> 00:17:02.089
calls within 60 seconds.

00:17:02.089 --> 00:17:06.189
The green line at the bottom shows where the
patients abandoned calls.

00:17:06.189 --> 00:17:12.822
That is now down to the sort of abandonment
rates that you would consider to be perfectly

00:17:12.822 --> 00:17:16.089
acceptable for a service of this nature.

00:17:16.089 --> 00:17:19.890
The other one that I think is really important
is that people have been really worried about

00:17:19.890 --> 00:17:23.780
111 in terms of handover to clinicians.

00:17:23.780 --> 00:17:26.089
The call handlers are lay call handlers.

00:17:26.089 --> 00:17:31.240
However, they do work with a very specific
algorithm that has been tried and tested over

00:17:31.240 --> 00:17:39.040
the years, and was created by the Royal Colleges,
and tested by them.

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:42.510
Obviously, on occasions, the patient needs
to speak to a clinician.

00:17:42.510 --> 00:17:54.770
You will see from the pie chart on the right
that when this slide was created, 80% of calls

00:17:54.770 --> 00:17:58.520
were completed by call handlers without reference
to a clinician.

00:17:58.520 --> 00:18:04.700
Of the 20% that were then handed on to a clinician,
three-quarters were handed straight across.

00:18:04.700 --> 00:18:09.280
Therefore, this was not about patients having
to be called back for the vast majority.

00:18:09.280 --> 00:18:14.510
Of the small percentage that were called back,
half of them were called within 10 minutes.

00:18:14.510 --> 00:18:18.780
We will not rest until everybody is called
back well within 10 minutes.

00:18:18.780 --> 00:18:26.470
I can report actually that from the figures
for 14th July, 2013, 90% rather than 80% were

00:18:26.470 --> 00:18:34.940
in a position where they were transferred
straight away and the vast majority of the

00:18:34.940 --> 00:18:37.299
remaining ones were called back within 10
minutes.

00:18:37.299 --> 00:18:40.650
I think performance has definitely improved
and stabilised.

00:18:40.650 --> 00:18:42.470
That is not a reason for complacency.

00:18:42.470 --> 00:18:48.580
It is a very big and very important service,
and one which we believe will be of enormous

00:18:48.580 --> 00:18:51.100
benefit to the public in the future.

00:18:51.100 --> 00:18:57.429
We have a full programme Board in place, which
is looking at making sure that we really have

00:18:57.429 --> 00:19:00.080
stabilised the system and got great delivery.

00:19:00.080 --> 00:19:03.970
We are looking at all the clinical and quality
aspects, and making sure that they are the

00:19:03.970 --> 00:19:05.360
best they can be for patients.

00:19:05.360 --> 00:19:06.720
We are also looking to the future.

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:08.250
There are three parts of that programme.

00:19:08.250 --> 00:19:12.630
We are looking to the future to see what strategic
direction should be for 111 and what we can

00:19:12.630 --> 00:19:15.570
do to make it even better.

00:19:15.570 --> 00:19:16.570
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much, Barbara.

00:19:16.570 --> 00:19:22.020
We know that the launch of 111 was not something
that we would have wished.

00:19:22.020 --> 00:19:27.059
We are coming back to that as an item later
on the agenda, when we look at how we will

00:19:27.059 --> 00:19:29.809
handle in the future major projects.

00:19:29.809 --> 00:19:35.030
This is one, of course, that we inherited
at the time of its launch.

00:19:35.030 --> 00:19:39.940
What I am interested to know, I suppose, is:
what do you anticipate to be the increase

00:19:39.940 --> 00:19:41.770
in take up of the use of 111?

00:19:41.770 --> 00:19:45.690
How are you projecting that forward?

00:19:45.690 --> 00:19:52.570
&gt;&gt; We are already seeing a greater uptake
of 111 than we first thought because of all

00:19:52.570 --> 00:19:54.510
the publicity.

00:19:54.510 --> 00:20:00.340
When 111 is introduced in an area, it has
what is called a soft launch, initially.

00:20:00.340 --> 00:20:03.900
In other words, it is launched without a blaze
of publicity.

00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:08.740
Generally, 111 takes two sorts of calls that
would have come in previously.

00:20:08.740 --> 00:20:14.200
One is the 0845 number, which was less about
urgent care and more about advice.

00:20:14.200 --> 00:20:17.390
The other part is the GP out of hours.

00:20:17.390 --> 00:20:22.100
In all areas, you do the soft launch first
whereby, if you ring either of those numbers,

00:20:22.100 --> 00:20:24.500
you will be just transferred through to 111.

00:20:24.500 --> 00:20:29.539
The service runs for a while in that locality
before that locality then starts to advertise

00:20:29.539 --> 00:20:32.610
to individuals that they can just dial 111.

00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:37.900
Of course, in a lot of places, that is already
happening.

00:20:37.900 --> 00:20:42.179
Actually, in 90% of the country -- in terms
of geographical coverage -- if you dial 111,

00:20:42.179 --> 00:20:48.809
you will get through to a local service, because
111 is active and if there is not an a 111

00:20:48.809 --> 00:20:53.510
call centre in place, you will be automatically
redirected to GP out of hours.

00:20:53.510 --> 00:20:56.539
We have 90% coverage already.

00:20:56.539 --> 00:21:01.020
The initial plan was that 111 would be rolled
out everywhere.

00:21:01.020 --> 00:21:04.380
Initially, the plan was the end of March.

00:21:04.380 --> 00:21:09.380
Many months ago, the Department of Health
offered areas the opportunity to delay and

00:21:09.380 --> 00:21:14.880
we did have a number of areas opting to delay
until the autumn of this year.

00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:20.390
With the latest issues that have gone on,
NHS Direct, who was one of the providers who

00:21:20.390 --> 00:21:27.130
really struggled, have informed us that they
will not be able to provide the services in

00:21:27.130 --> 00:21:33.210
two areas that they were due to take up in
the autumn, which is Cornwall and Essex.

00:21:33.210 --> 00:21:35.540
We are now in the process of supporting CCGs.

00:21:35.540 --> 00:21:40.700
I forgot to say that we support CCGs who are
the commissioners of this service.

00:21:40.700 --> 00:21:44.350
We are supporting CCGs to seek alternative
providers.

00:21:44.350 --> 00:21:49.549
We have agreed on that this basis that in
these two areas the launch can be delayed

00:21:49.549 --> 00:21:50.750
even after the autumn.

00:21:50.750 --> 00:21:57.690
It is likely to be in the early part of 2014,
simply because we think that ensuring that

00:21:57.690 --> 00:22:03.440
this service is absolutely safe before it
is rolled out anywhere else is critical.

00:22:03.440 --> 00:22:11.539
In every area when there is either a new rollout
or an increase of 111 breadth, then actually

00:22:11.539 --> 00:22:15.720
the programme Board, which I chair, looks
at that very specifically to make sure that

00:22:15.720 --> 00:22:21.670
it is ready to go.

00:22:21.670 --> 00:22:26.520
&gt;&gt; I just wanted to ask Barbara if I may,
Malcolm, about the second bullet which you

00:22:26.520 --> 00:22:27.520
have been talking about.

00:22:27.520 --> 00:22:34.120
It mentions only 5% of callers compared to
70% under NHS Direct need to be called back.

00:22:34.120 --> 00:22:42.760
Is this simply down to the quality of their
algorithm?

00:22:42.760 --> 00:22:49.620
Because some of the criticism in the media
has been about the number of lay call handlers

00:22:49.620 --> 00:22:53.990
rather than nurses or other professionals
really.

00:22:53.990 --> 00:22:56.320
The satisfaction rates are very good.

00:22:56.320 --> 00:23:01.800
That is an amazing difference really, but
is it just that algorithm, or is there something

00:23:01.800 --> 00:23:05.490
else that is making so significant a difference.

00:23:05.490 --> 00:23:10.780
&gt;&gt; Sorry, my eye was instantly drawn when
you said the second bullet point to the 'principle'

00:23:10.780 --> 00:23:12.679
which has somehow crept it.

00:23:12.679 --> 00:23:16.170
It is wrong and spelt incorrectly.

00:23:16.170 --> 00:23:18.120
I do apologise.

00:23:18.120 --> 00:23:26.250
I think one of the reasons why things improve
over weeks is the experience of the call handlers.

00:23:26.250 --> 00:23:31.420
All the call handlers are trained and they
all use the same algorithm and it is an algorithm

00:23:31.420 --> 00:23:33.250
that is used not only by 111.

00:23:33.250 --> 00:23:38.000
It is a highly acclaimed algorithm for lay
call handlers.

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:43.210
We also need to remember that it is not only
111 who does not have a clinician taking calls.

00:23:43.210 --> 00:23:45.050
Ambulance services do not.

00:23:45.050 --> 00:23:47.460
Most GP surgeries initially will have calls
taken by receptionists.

00:23:47.460 --> 00:23:49.740
There are lots of areas.

00:23:49.740 --> 00:23:58.240
I think that the improvement in the time has
been as those call handlers have become more

00:23:58.240 --> 00:23:59.240
experienced.

00:23:59.240 --> 00:24:03.600
That actually then starts to allow them to
manage the calls much more swiftly and efficiently

00:24:03.600 --> 00:24:06.350
and that gives a better experience for the
patients.

00:24:06.350 --> 00:24:10.280
Obviously, it means they can answer other
calls.

00:24:10.280 --> 00:24:14.409
We have seen in every site that has rolled
out that as a few weeks have gone by, they

00:24:14.409 --> 00:24:15.409
have got much better.

00:24:15.409 --> 00:24:21.279
We do have very high satisfaction rates, but
I think we need to be cautious about the satisfaction

00:24:21.279 --> 00:24:29.250
rates, in that there is a bit of a time lag
in their reporting and actually the time when

00:24:29.250 --> 00:24:34.130
we were delivering the worst service to our
patients was in March, and I think there is

00:24:34.130 --> 00:24:35.630
still a bit of time to see that.

00:24:35.630 --> 00:24:40.230
I suspect that we will see a deterioration
in satisfaction and then, hopefully, improvements

00:24:40.230 --> 00:24:41.230
again.

00:24:41.230 --> 00:24:42.230
Did that answer the question?

00:24:42.230 --> 00:24:43.230
&gt;&gt; I think so.

00:24:43.230 --> 00:24:44.230
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:24:44.230 --> 00:24:45.230
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much.

00:24:45.230 --> 00:24:50.190
Now, still on this item, I would like to invite
to Paul to talk about financial performance.

00:24:50.190 --> 00:24:52.210
&gt;&gt; I will keep this brief.

00:24:52.210 --> 00:25:06.910
I think I just want to begin by paying tribute
to the finance team.

00:25:06.910 --> 00:25:09.840
It would be worth our while noting that today.

00:25:09.840 --> 00:25:12.720
The first big challenge has been closing down
the old world.

00:25:12.720 --> 00:25:18.610
I have to say that I had some sense of anxiety
-- as it were -- going into that process because

00:25:18.610 --> 00:25:22.230
it is always complicated when there is not
another year to pick up the things that were

00:25:22.230 --> 00:25:24.600
not done quite right in the final year.

00:25:24.600 --> 00:25:28.730
It is true to say that that close-down process
has gone exceptionally well.

00:25:28.730 --> 00:25:33.680
It has been one of the least problematic year-end
close-downs that I have experienced in my

00:25:33.680 --> 00:25:35.360
time in the NHS.

00:25:35.360 --> 00:25:38.240
As a first achievement, I think that is a
very big one.

00:25:38.240 --> 00:25:41.409
The second thing is, as I have said before
in the Board meetings, that this year's planning

00:25:41.409 --> 00:25:45.770
process is certainly the most difficult that
I have personally been involved in.

00:25:45.770 --> 00:25:47.279
There are two real reasons for that.

00:25:47.279 --> 00:25:51.940
One is the structural change itself, which
has given us all sorts of challenges of splitting

00:25:51.940 --> 00:25:56.919
out specialised from less-specialised acute
care, and all those other challenges.

00:25:56.919 --> 00:26:01.130
The second reason is that this is a really
difficult year to plan for because of the

00:26:01.130 --> 00:26:04.980
inherent challenges of economic circumstances
and the other challenges.

00:26:04.980 --> 00:26:08.850
It is good to be able to report that we have
now got to the point in the planning process

00:26:08.850 --> 00:26:13.040
where we can close off that process and move
into managing the year.

00:26:13.040 --> 00:26:14.870
You might think it is about time too.

00:26:14.870 --> 00:26:19.040
It is certainly really important that we can
switch across from that.

00:26:19.040 --> 00:26:21.630
The third thing is something we must not underestimate.

00:26:21.630 --> 00:26:28.649
Every one of the 212 commissioning organisations
around the country has had to cope with a

00:26:28.649 --> 00:26:33.700
completely new accounting system, a completely
new structure of accounts, a completely new

00:26:33.700 --> 00:26:38.309
set of reporting requirements, etc. whilst
dealing with those first two things.

00:26:38.309 --> 00:26:45.130
The fact that there is a page 10 at the back
of section C, which is the performance report,

00:26:45.130 --> 00:26:47.060
is frankly little short of a miracle.

00:26:47.060 --> 00:26:53.020
It is not a perfect page 10, I would say,
in the sense that there is a variety of other

00:26:53.020 --> 00:26:57.110
information that we are going to bring from
month three onwards, which complement looking

00:26:57.110 --> 00:27:00.049
at whether we spent more than we expected
to or not.

00:27:00.049 --> 00:27:04.179
I would say that there are a handful of organisations
who still, within the numbers you are looking

00:27:04.179 --> 00:27:08.070
at here, have got the odd little glitch they
still need to sort out, which will come through

00:27:08.070 --> 00:27:09.070
in month three.

00:27:09.070 --> 00:27:14.610
I just thought it was worth putting that on
record because it is no mean feat what the

00:27:14.610 --> 00:27:17.539
teams have been able to achieve.

00:27:17.539 --> 00:27:22.539
Having said all of which, I would advise you
to enjoy month two with a degree of caution,

00:27:22.539 --> 00:27:26.160
partly for the reasons I have just talked
about in terms the novelty of the accounting

00:27:26.160 --> 00:27:30.510
processes, and more importantly because in
month two, for a commissioning organisation,

00:27:30.510 --> 00:27:35.250
you can virtually never tell very much because
you have only got really one month of solid

00:27:35.250 --> 00:27:36.899
data and the rest is estimation.

00:27:36.899 --> 00:27:43.710
Whenever you have a large quantity of estimation,
it is dangerous to extrapolate from trends

00:27:43.710 --> 00:27:45.289
you can see coming through.

00:27:45.289 --> 00:27:50.870
The fact that we are apparently according
to this slightly underspent accumulative to

00:27:50.870 --> 00:27:55.940
month two -- and I hope to get a consistent
pattern for anything you see which has not

00:27:55.940 --> 00:27:58.870
got red or brackets around it -- is good news.

00:27:58.870 --> 00:28:03.419
The fact that we are marginally underspent
in month two and that we are not showing significant

00:28:03.419 --> 00:28:08.010
variances in the forecast outlook is good
that it does not have any indicators of negativity

00:28:08.010 --> 00:28:12.460
around it, but it is not a reason to think
that we are through the woods and out the

00:28:12.460 --> 00:28:13.460
other side.

00:28:13.460 --> 00:28:14.460
We are far from it.

00:28:14.460 --> 00:28:18.529
I anticipate being able to bring to you much
more interesting financial reports in future

00:28:18.529 --> 00:28:22.080
months for all sorts of good and bad reasons.

00:28:22.080 --> 00:28:26.280
I would advise you to -- I would say -- just
enjoy these numbers with a degree of caution.

00:28:26.280 --> 00:28:35.190
&gt;&gt; I have one comment and one question.

00:28:35.190 --> 00:28:41.290
The comment is really that going forward I
think it would be helpful to be able to see

00:28:41.290 --> 00:28:48.340
the costs that relate to NHS England that
are the direct costs that we manage, so the

00:28:48.340 --> 00:28:56.980
6,500 people in establishments etc., because
they get drowned out by these very large numbers.

00:28:56.980 --> 00:29:02.140
That is really the thing that we are responsible
for directly managing.

00:29:02.140 --> 00:29:05.799
I think it would be helpful to see that broken
out.

00:29:05.799 --> 00:29:10.789
The other question was just to amplify on
your point about estimates.

00:29:10.789 --> 00:29:16.830
The places where there are quite big numbers
and the plan and actual are identical, is

00:29:16.830 --> 00:29:22.019
that because they are estimates or they really
are identical?

00:29:22.019 --> 00:29:25.390
&gt;&gt; It depends which columns you are looking
at.

00:29:25.390 --> 00:29:29.419
Let me deal with the second question first,
and I will come back to the first afterwards.

00:29:29.419 --> 00:29:35.110
In terms of the forecast outlook, it is absolutely
normal at this stage of the year, unless some

00:29:35.110 --> 00:29:39.010
calamity has taken place, to see those figures
in-line with each other.

00:29:39.010 --> 00:29:43.179
In the year to date it is essentially -- remember
what I said -- that for the first month we

00:29:43.179 --> 00:29:44.179
have hard data to go with.

00:29:44.179 --> 00:29:49.170
These are two months' of numbers, so typically
there will be an estimation for the second

00:29:49.170 --> 00:29:50.960
month which puts it there.

00:29:50.960 --> 00:29:54.840
If there is not a significant trend one way
or the other, it would not at all surprise

00:29:54.840 --> 00:29:56.970
me if most of them were in-line with plan.

00:29:56.970 --> 00:30:01.929
If it gives you any comfort -- it may not
do -- I looked back at the month two reporting

00:30:01.929 --> 00:30:05.940
from the old system last year, and it was
a very, very consistent pattern with what

00:30:05.940 --> 00:30:06.940
you are seeing here.

00:30:06.940 --> 00:30:10.600
There was only one organisation last year
which was showing any significant variance

00:30:10.600 --> 00:30:12.390
at all at month two from its plan.

00:30:12.390 --> 00:30:15.010
Therefore, it is a fairly typical profile.

00:30:15.010 --> 00:30:20.800
To go back to your first question, in the
full set that I talked about that we have

00:30:20.800 --> 00:30:25.210
got for month three, there are five or six
charts on finance.

00:30:25.210 --> 00:30:26.210
I cannot remember exactly how many.

00:30:26.210 --> 00:30:30.350
One of those charts is a very specific directorate
by directorate, programme by programme look

00:30:30.350 --> 00:30:31.590
at the internal costs.

00:30:31.590 --> 00:30:36.820
It comes into the section which I think we
call 'organisational health' section D, which

00:30:36.820 --> 00:30:37.960
comes after this.

00:30:37.960 --> 00:30:42.500
We have not got that fine information available
for month two, for all the reasons I talk

00:30:42.500 --> 00:30:43.500
about.

00:30:43.500 --> 00:30:46.259
We will have that for month three.

00:30:46.259 --> 00:30:52.730
&gt;&gt; In aggregate, where you do not have it
split by area, are we more or less on track

00:30:52.730 --> 00:30:55.570
for the own establish costs?

00:30:55.570 --> 00:30:59.691
&gt;&gt; Yes, we are at month two, with all the
caveats I gave earlier on.

00:30:59.691 --> 00:31:09.330
&gt;&gt; On the financial side, I know that Paul
commented that the staff had to be commended

00:31:09.330 --> 00:31:17.899
for getting to where they got to, and also
I note Paul's warning that there may be some

00:31:17.899 --> 00:31:23.180
things yet to come, particularly in terms
of the year-end cutover and transition.

00:31:23.180 --> 00:31:28.049
The wider point, Tim, is that this is tremendous
progress.

00:31:28.049 --> 00:31:33.260
I think that once it is online and we can
look at it on a layered basis, and we can

00:31:33.260 --> 00:31:39.210
start doing some of this exception reporting,
this dispersion tracking from the mean, it

00:31:39.210 --> 00:31:40.210
will be great.

00:31:40.210 --> 00:31:45.889
The point that is also important -- I think
-- for the Board is that we have a mechanism

00:31:45.889 --> 00:31:51.250
whereby we are very clear that the information
is being assured and therefore what we are

00:31:51.250 --> 00:31:59.080
looking at is data that is accurate, complete
and has had a systems-based assurance over

00:31:59.080 --> 00:32:00.080
it.

00:32:00.080 --> 00:32:03.380
This is going to become vital decision-making
information.

00:32:03.380 --> 00:32:12.880
I would look to the internal audit function
having an oversight, having a look at the

00:32:12.880 --> 00:32:17.279
generation of this data from source to Board,
in a number of areas.

00:32:17.279 --> 00:32:23.220
I will report back, I think we must report
back on that later in the year, when that

00:32:23.220 --> 00:32:24.220
work is done.

00:32:24.220 --> 00:32:29.750
&gt;&gt; I think we can sum up by saying that this
is a major step forward, Tim, and we are appreciative.

00:32:29.750 --> 00:32:35.299
We take onboard your initial comment that
some of the data is very useful because it

00:32:35.299 --> 00:32:40.290
is timely and it is ours, and some of it is
not very useful at all but it gives us some

00:32:40.290 --> 00:32:45.650
sort of potentially unreliable historical
background, but as the year goes by and as

00:32:45.650 --> 00:32:49.860
the quality of the data and the direct applicability
of the data both improve, we will have -- I

00:32:49.860 --> 00:32:54.740
think -- high quality reporting, with all
of Ed's reservations about ensuring that the

00:32:54.740 --> 00:32:57.780
data itself and its quality is assured.

00:32:57.780 --> 00:33:04.549
I think we also take on Paul's point.

00:33:04.549 --> 00:33:08.789
Sometimes it is easy for the non-executives
simply to forget how complex has been the

00:33:08.789 --> 00:33:14.360
process of underlying change and how much
work has gone into this across the whole of

00:33:14.360 --> 00:33:16.580
the organisation.

00:33:16.580 --> 00:33:19.679
To have our attention drawn to the fact that
everybody is working with a completely new

00:33:19.679 --> 00:33:24.799
accounting system and new accounting standards
is quite a remarkable change, I think.

00:33:24.799 --> 00:33:30.200
For the first time, we have actually got the
ability to capture all the information for

00:33:30.200 --> 00:33:31.270
the system as a whole.

00:33:31.270 --> 00:33:32.649
I think that is a major step forward.

00:33:32.649 --> 00:33:36.360
It will take a while, I think, for the systems
to develop.

00:33:36.360 --> 00:33:42.720
It will take a while for us a Board to understand
what is the best information that we need

00:33:42.720 --> 00:33:45.830
to provide us with the assurance on a meeting
by meeting basis.

00:33:45.830 --> 00:33:51.909
We will work with you over time to bring ourselves
to that position.

00:33:51.909 --> 00:34:02.240
&gt;&gt; The only comment I was going to make was,
the parity of esteem between mental and physical

00:34:02.240 --> 00:34:20.210
health flag up that it is something that we
need to measure.

00:34:20.210 --> 00:34:25.590
I do not know how we get there to be honest.

00:34:25.590 --> 00:34:29.720
On the question of understanding the complexity,
which is an indication of the amount of work

00:34:29.720 --> 00:34:35.619
that has gone into it generally, on the mental
health esteem stuff I am just not clear.

00:34:35.619 --> 00:34:42.360
&gt;&gt; It draws broadly on the equalities piece,
which is critical to our success.

00:34:42.360 --> 00:34:47.760
Actually, I would like to just echo Paul's
point that this reflects incredible work by

00:34:47.760 --> 00:34:53.260
a group of people right across the organisation,
led by Richard Murray, our unbelievably great

00:34:53.260 --> 00:34:54.780
chief analyst.

00:34:54.780 --> 00:35:01.160
Just the act of doing this has raised all
sorts of really vital issues around how we

00:35:01.160 --> 00:35:06.130
do hold ourselves operationally to account
and, actually, the very complex question of

00:35:06.130 --> 00:35:09.830
how we measure inequalities is one of the
core questions.

00:35:09.830 --> 00:35:14.950
I am really hoping that as quickly as possible,
and certainly over the next few months, we

00:35:14.950 --> 00:35:18.310
will be coming back with a much enhanced view
of both the methodology for measuring some

00:35:18.310 --> 00:35:21.780
of these things and subsequently the data
to support the measures.

00:35:21.780 --> 00:35:24.440
It is work in progress.

00:35:24.440 --> 00:35:31.040
&gt;&gt; We are, are we not, on the way to having
a globally unique system of reporting and

00:35:31.040 --> 00:35:32.040
of data?

00:35:32.040 --> 00:35:35.610
I do not know of any other system in the world
that could produce this level of data, and

00:35:35.610 --> 00:35:38.600
not just produce it but have it in the public
domain.

00:35:38.600 --> 00:35:42.830
&gt;&gt; Malcolm, I have just one more point that
reinforces that.

00:35:42.830 --> 00:35:47.830
It does not come through in the data but this
is national data.

00:35:47.830 --> 00:35:53.700
Because we do not have volumes, but have percentages,
we do not actually see the scale that we are

00:35:53.700 --> 00:35:54.870
actually reporting on.

00:35:54.870 --> 00:36:00.040
It might be quite helpful, from time to time,
to build the scale of this in terms of the

00:36:00.040 --> 00:36:03.280
absolute numbers of people who are going to
Accident and Emergency, and so on.

00:36:03.280 --> 00:36:04.360
You have got it?

00:36:04.360 --> 00:36:07.350
&gt;&gt; Yes, I think the online point you made
earlier is very important.

00:36:07.350 --> 00:36:10.610
I think probably what we need to do -- and
I will talk to Richard and Grant Lewis, who

00:36:10.610 --> 00:36:15.590
has also been very involved as to how we can
bring non-executive members of the Board up

00:36:15.590 --> 00:36:20.070
to speed on what is already available online,
would I think help with some of those questions.

00:36:20.070 --> 00:36:26.270
&gt;&gt; I have just one point in response to Victor's
question, which I think is a really good one.

00:36:26.270 --> 00:36:31.690
That is just to give you the reassurance that
on some of those more difficult to measure

00:36:31.690 --> 00:36:36.640
areas, like parity of esteem for mental health,
we are not just waiting until we can define

00:36:36.640 --> 00:36:37.640
a measure.

00:36:37.640 --> 00:36:42.460
There are strategies and great clinical leadership
work to try define that programme, working

00:36:42.460 --> 00:36:49.210
with different patient groups to start saying,
what would 'great' look like, so we can put

00:36:49.210 --> 00:36:54.840
some actions in place that we can measure
as we develop the outcome measure at the same

00:36:54.840 --> 00:36:55.840
time.

00:36:55.840 --> 00:36:56.840
&gt;&gt; Good.

00:36:56.840 --> 00:36:59.450
Tim, Barbara and Paul, thank you very much
for that item.

00:36:59.450 --> 00:37:01.980
None of this ambition comes without risk,
so I think we need now to turn to item eight,

00:37:01.980 --> 00:37:02.980
which is the Board Assurance Framework.

00:37:02.980 --> 00:37:02.986
Bill, will you please introduce this.

